Minimizers: converge on the largest per-atom gradient, not the smallest

SteepestDescentTakeNSteps, ConjugateGradientsTakeNSteps and the L-BFGS
gradient gather each pair the energy criterion (|dE| < econv) with a
per-atom gradient one (|g|^2 < _gconv = 0.01), but tracked the *minimum*
|g|^2 over the atoms. One atom at rest -- typically a hydrogen -- was then
enough to stop the run with the rest of the molecule still under load:
[NH4+].CC(=O)[O-] under UFF stopped "converged" after a few hundred CG
steps at E ~ +1.3..+2, against a minimum near -0.64. CG also measured the
search direction (gradient plus beta times the previous direction) instead
of the gradient.

Track the maximum instead, over the gradient as the minimizer sees it
(fixed atoms and fixed axes excluded, so a restrained axis cannot block
convergence), and count a non-finite gradient as not at rest.

